  "worked_solution": "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =2.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that its augmented matrix $M$ is already in row echelon form:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} \\\\  & =2(\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{3})+3{x}_{3} \\\\  & =6{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}6{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=2,$ we get the eigenvector\n$v=[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$",
